crap, I gotta get outta this?

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.720
- I think when I was 10 years old.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.950
I remember, ah, like something
happened where it seemed like

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.640
really desperate and had
an epiphany that if things

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.185
don't go in a different direction

00:03:24.185 --> 00:03:25.357
they're not gonna end well.

00:03:25.357 --> 00:03:26.581
And I remember--

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:27.414
- What year were you born?

00:03:27.414 --> 00:03:28.600
- I was 1974.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.703
- Got it.

00:03:29.703 --> 00:03:30.536
So in '84--

00:03:30.536 --> 00:03:31.369
- 84--
- Okay.

00:03:31.369 --> 00:03:34.100
- I, a bunch of things
happened in my mother's life,

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.116
you know, she was sick--

00:03:35.116 --> 00:03:35.949
- Like well--

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:38.860
- She was very depressed and just sick.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.160
- Did she date other guys?

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.870
- No, no, we grew up--
- That's interesting.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:42.703
- Yeah it's interesting you ask that.

00:03:42.703 --> 00:03:45.670
Yeah, we were kind of a
unit and for some reason

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.060
around that time, I remember
I had been in a bus accident

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.140
and I was standing at the
ocean looking at the waves

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.730
thinking okay, this is not gonna end well.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.180
And I kind of, I think that
was the beginning of me

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.360
being a little bit of a
loner and deciding like,

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.429
I need to figure out a
way out of this situation.

00:03:59.429 --> 00:04:00.585
- And when you say a loner, do you,

00:04:00.585 --> 00:04:01.418
this is just an interesting question.

00:04:01.418 --> 00:04:03.180
I mean, we, this is what's fun about this.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.827
I've never really gone
into these places with you.

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:05.950
- I'm getting uncomfortable.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.729
- I know, you should because
I'm about to go there.

00:04:07.729 --> 00:04:08.640
(Matt laughs)

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.880
Loner as in like, okay, not
that I don't want to be like

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.740
my brothers, but when you're
number four, and I'm listening

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:16.920
and I've never thought about it this way,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.160
but we're putting on a show here.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.242
You wanted to be on the show, here you go.

00:04:20.242 --> 00:04:22.880
Do you mean loner, like, and
by the way, it's interesting

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.390
'cause I have a version of this too.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.240
Which is like, and I've (knocks
purposefully) had such good

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.710
things that I'm dealing, it's
just like, sometimes you go

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:29.660
inside of your head--

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.060
- Yeah, actually I've never
thought about what do I mean

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.310
by that word, but now I do.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.440
We used to go to the
emergency room all the time

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.660
for healthcare 'cause we had nothing,

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.400
and that became kind of our
doctor, and it's the time

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.020
when I started realizing
that these were intractable

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.000
problems, you don't use
the word attend but like,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.560
there's no answer to this.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.750
And that you have to kind of
take matters into your own

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.250
hands, not like I had the answers at 10,

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.920
but I definitely had the problem at 10.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:55.753
- [Gary] Yes.

00:04:55.753 --> 00:04:58.760
- And then something happened,
back to pattern recognition.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.340
When I was a kid, my
mother actually got a GED

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.370
as an adult.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:03.610
And she was able to go to college.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:04.830
She did really well.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.410
And around the time I was in eighth grade,

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:07.540
I said wait a second.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.040
If she was able to go
to college as an adult,

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.930
kinda by accident, if
I did this on purpose,

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.540
then I could get out of
this situation even faster.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.050
- So she, so listen.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.830
I grew up at the same
time as you did and school

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:20.050
became a gateway out.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.090
- Yeah, school became a gateway
out, but I also unnecessary.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.380
Like, it just didn't add up.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.051
Like, okay, I have a
desperate circumstance,

00:05:26.051 --> 00:05:28.790
we're very poor, we need to move quickly,

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:32.270
she's getting progressively sicker, right?

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.710
And then there's this side
path which is if you actually

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:38.150
drop out at 16 and do well
enough, you can go to college.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.920
So that was probably the
first most unconventional

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.890
decision that I ever made, that
everybody said you're crazy

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.690
like, you're throwing it away.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.420
And when I say loner, it's the,
when I started to cultivate

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.220
this voice in my head
like hey, wait a minute,

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.637
I have a plan and I don't need
anybody else to agree to it

00:05:52.637 --> 00:05:55.079
because they're not
looking at the apartment.

00:05:55.079 --> 00:05:56.341
- They don't have context.

00:05:56.341 --> 00:05:57.174
- They don't have context.

00:05:57.174 --> 00:05:58.007
You always say all the time, which I love,

00:05:58.007 --> 00:05:59.630
nobody had a window.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.489
First of all, when you're a
kid, you're trying to wear

00:06:01.489 --> 00:06:02.580
Jordash Jeans or Guess.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:03.710
I'm dating myself, right?
(Gary laughing)

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.070
You're doing everything you
can to conceal your shame.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:06.903
- [Gary] Sure.

00:06:06.903 --> 00:06:08.260
- So, everybody was saying
you're insane to drop out

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:09.230
of high school, I was
like no, you don't know

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:10.320
what you're talking about.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.633
Now when I made the move and I dropped out

00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:14.628
of Cazorla High School at
16 and I was like okay,

00:06:14.628 --> 00:06:15.510
now you gotta--

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:17.990
- Which is so, right now if
people drop out of high school,

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.300
like in 2018, 2019,
and they've got a plan?

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.710
There's a level of like oh,
that might be an entrepreneur

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.360
that's gonna go do something.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.600
When you and I were growing
up, anybody that dropped out

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.280
of high school was 100% certified loser.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.170
- Oh forget about it, right.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.780
And that's funny, I remember when I--

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.166
- Like, you even just went
back to New York Talk,

00:06:35.166 --> 00:06:36.210
just forget about it.
- Oh no, when I,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.372
when I, did I say forget--
- It like set you back,

00:06:38.372 --> 00:06:39.455
(men laughing)

00:06:39.455 --> 00:06:40.949
that was the most Queensy thing--

00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:42.226
- No I have Queens--
- I've been hanging,

00:06:42.226 --> 00:06:44.160
that was the most Queensy,
like, it literally just,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.530
that moment just put you back in there.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.630
- So, I remember, or now
I'm gonna take it real.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.400
I remember sitting on the
steps, first of all you have to

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.520
return all your textbooks when
you drop out of high school.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.353
Back then, at least.

00:06:53.353 --> 00:06:54.450
So you have to go class to class.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.750
And I remember walking into
my science teacher's class,

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.760
Mr. Rosenthaw if you're
out there, it stuck,

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.950
and I went to bring back my
textbook and he goes, like,

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:04.220
Higgins, what a shame, what a waste.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.730
And he goes, I remember
he told me, I'll see you

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:08.810
at McDonald's, at the time
I did work at McDonald's.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:10.970
I said well, if you see me at
McDonald's because I own it,

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:11.803
that was all talk.

00:07:11.803 --> 00:07:13.830
I went out, sat on the steps
of Cardoza, smoked a butt

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.900
'cause now I was gonna get
picked up by the truant police

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.360
at that point, and I thought damn.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.553
Like, that was a balsy move.

00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:22.860
- And so what happened next?

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:23.760
- I executed.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.208
- Okay.
- I said, I--

00:07:25.208 --> 00:07:26.041
- So what did you do?
- I know I'm right.

00:07:26.041 --> 00:07:28.990
I went, I started taking
GED classes for like a week,

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:30.700
and I was like alright,
this is not for me.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.320
You're allowed to take it
on standby, if you show up.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.550
I went to Springfield Gardens
High School in Queens,

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:40.180
I went on standby, took
my GED, took the SAT's

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:43.010
a couple of weeks later, and
within three months I enrolled

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:44.420
in Queens College at 16.

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:45.253
- Okay.

00:07:45.253 --> 00:07:47.940
- Then, just to prove a
point, went back to my prom

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.307
at high school as president
of the debate team,

00:07:49.307 --> 00:07:50.940
and I was like how do
you like them apples,

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.065
to date myself.

00:07:52.065 --> 00:07:53.080
(Matt laughing)

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:54.757
So, to me that's probably one
of the most important things

00:07:54.757 --> 00:07:56.270
that's ever happened in my life.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:59.240
Because, everything you talk
about, you don't have context

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.070
so I'm not gonna listen to
you because your opinion

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:03.082
doesn't matter 'cause you
don't have the full story.

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:03.983
- I mean, that is just--

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:04.880
- Right?

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:05.910
- I have a plan.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.830
And if I have a plan and
I believe in the plan,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.690
that's more important than
anything else that's going on,

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:10.523
right?
- Sure.

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:12.860
- And, I have pattern
recognition skills that maybe

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:13.693
set me apart.

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:15.970
So, trust in them, right,
that they can get you

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:16.990
where you want to go.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:18.000
And your point about how you get judged

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.150
about being a dropout?

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:23.140
I knew that I'm gonna have
to make sure that the rest

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.720
of the track record can
overcome that one decision.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.030
- Well, it's like being
a sixth round pick.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:28.863
- [Matt] Right.

00:08:28.863 --> 00:08:30.160
- You're judged for
being a sixth round pick

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:31.400
until you go to the Hall of Fame.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:32.503
- Exactly, well, it's funny you said,

00:08:32.503 --> 00:08:34.314
like law school is my Hall of Fame.

00:08:34.314 --> 00:08:36.269
'Cause I was like this,
okay, I'll go to law school--

00:08:36.269 --> 00:08:37.770
- Makes sense.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.540
- I'll do is, I was in law
review, top in the class,

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:42.380
whatever, I went through
four years of law school

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.940
at night, and as soon as I
graduated, I never even took

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.190
the bar, I said okay,
well I cleaned that up.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.243
- And then what happened?

00:08:48.243 --> 00:08:51.169
- Well, all in between
my career was taking off.

00:08:51.169 --> 00:08:54.710
So, because I dropped out
early, got great jobs early.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:55.543
- Like what?

00:08:55.543 --> 00:08:56.376
Like I actually--

00:08:56.376 --> 00:08:57.209
- You don't know?

00:08:57.209 --> 00:08:58.042
So--
- No.

00:08:59.141 --> 00:09:00.890
- While I was doing that,
I was working as a reporter

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:02.220
for the Queens Tribune

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.720
and people would send me their problems.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.359
I created a little column
called The Trib Action Desk,

00:09:06.359 --> 00:09:08.440
and they would send me
their problems about things

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.240
they were do, and they
were mundane issues.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.500
But I would take the mundane
and make it very interesting.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:16.540
You know, some guy in Hamilton
Beach, Queens and the city

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.040
had built a bridge next to
his house and the bridge

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.350
had started destroying the
foundation and he had an animal

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.210
menagerie, and it ended up
blowing up in New York papers.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:26.900
Like, things like, I would
take these crazy stories,

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:28.470
I'd drive around in my
little Nissan Maxima

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.310
with the broken radiator
like, all night long--

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:31.640
- And who took note of that--

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:33.540
- Uh, a lot of different reporters.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.167
Daily News did a big profile on me,

00:09:35.167 --> 00:09:37.720
called me a real action hero.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:39.720
And it started happening.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:40.990
Started happening.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:42.080
And then ultimately,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.520
Mayor Giuliani gave me my first big job.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.229
- What were you doing before Giuliani?

00:09:46.229 --> 00:09:48.680
- I was doing doing the--

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.173
- Didn't you do like a
.com thing, like Kozmo?

00:09:50.173 --> 00:09:51.400
- That was in between.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:52.250
- [Gary] Yeah, what was that?

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.310
- Um, Kozmo.com.
- Yeah, Kozmo, yeah.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.600
- [Matt] Still amazing.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:56.450
- [Gary] You worth a trillion dollars--

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:57.820
- I was worth a trillion,
I mean, it was amazing,

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:58.653
I was so rich.

00:09:58.653 --> 00:10:00.185
10,000 options.
- Help me right here.

00:10:00.185 --> 00:10:03.420
'Cause this is where all the
kids, like, I just can't wait

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.380
for the world to melt, as you know.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.260
- I don't like when you say that.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:07.903
- I know, you get worried.
- We own this together.

00:10:07.903 --> 00:10:09.420
- I know, but like, I'm very--

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:11.244
- I'm still, I still have like PTSD--

00:10:11.244 --> 00:10:12.865
- Yes, I know, but I'm ready.
- The mattress.

00:10:12.865 --> 00:10:13.698
Yeah, weirdly--
- 'Cause I'm in the trenches.

00:10:13.698 --> 00:10:14.760
- [Matt] Well, I think you
just want to get back there.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:15.593
- I'm like, no no.

00:10:15.593 --> 00:10:16.590
I don't use it.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.190
I want to navigate the storm.

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:20.000
- Yeah, you like the big reveal.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.010
I'm done with the big reveal.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:22.970
- No, I get it, but here's the
good news in this scenario.

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:24.520
You got your own other
things, but you'll be able

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.210
to watch, I'll be like
this head right here,

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.890
watch this!
- Here, watch that, alright.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.390
- You know, right now a lot
of kids are either doing well,

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.810
I was at a conference
yesterday and I talked

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:35.950
where it's a lot of
videographers, a lot of vloggers,

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.640
a lot of influencers, I'm
like guys, you do understand

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.830
the $39000 you're making
this year in brand deals

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:45.012
on Instagram photos goes away
when the economy gets tight?

00:10:45.012 --> 00:10:47.550
Because, Coca-Cola gets tight.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.410
- It's cruel of you to
tell 'em it's happening.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.400
- No, it's the single best
thing I can do, right?

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:52.861
Because everybody's
living in fantasy land.

00:10:52.861 --> 00:10:53.694
- [Matt] That's true.

00:10:53.694 --> 00:10:55.880
- You lived in fantasy
land with your Kozmo stock,

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.440
and already thought about
what was gonna happen

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:58.980
and it all went to dead zero.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:00.838
- Well, that's what I was
about to, I learned something

00:11:00.838 --> 00:11:01.671
with Kosmo--

00:11:01.671 --> 00:11:02.504
- [Gary] That's good.

00:11:02.504 --> 00:11:03.337
Teach, teach, Matt.
- That was so important.

00:11:03.337 --> 00:11:04.550
So I got to Kos, okay I will.

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:06.380
Kozmo, one of the biggest
internet companies at the time

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.200
raised 300 million dollars in 1998.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:10.620
It would deliver everything

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:11.730
to you in under--
- Listen, Andy.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:13.290
- Deliver everything to
you in under an hour.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:14.500
It was actually an amazing company.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:15.487
- [Gary] Yeah, last mile.

00:11:15.487 --> 00:11:17.217
- Last mile, 10 thousand
employees, 10 cities--

00:11:17.217 --> 00:11:19.156
- By the way, here we are 20 years later,

00:11:19.156 --> 00:11:20.159
still no last mile.
- Still no last mile.

00:11:20.159 --> 00:11:21.771
- Another thing people need to learn.

00:11:21.771 --> 00:11:23.130
- And interesting that the
business could have been

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.950
profitable at an average order
of $24, 2.5 orders per hour,

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.954
and there was no smart
phone making those orders.

00:11:28.954 --> 00:11:29.787
- That's right.

00:11:29.787 --> 00:11:31.565
- That's just, anyway,
it's burned into my head.

00:11:31.565 --> 00:11:33.550
But what was interesting,
the signs were everywhere

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:34.383
towards the end.

00:11:34.383 --> 00:11:37.810
And I remember thinking we got,
somebody joined the company

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.233
as our CFO at the time and he had huge--

00:11:40.233 --> 00:11:41.066
- And what were you doing?

00:11:41.066 --> 00:11:42.250
- I was Director of Comms.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:43.411
- [Gary] Keep going.

00:11:43.411 --> 00:11:44.244
- So I did the road show, the whole thing.

00:11:44.244 --> 00:11:46.240
So this, I won't say who
the person was but they quit

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.900
this massive job at a moment
when I'm saying the signs

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:51.950
are everywhere that
we're about to go under,

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:53.261
and was completely seduced by--

00:11:53.261 --> 00:11:55.130
- The upside of the equity.

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:58.230
- And here I am, Giuliani
offers me a chance to become

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.211
the youngest Press Secretary
in New York City history

00:12:00.211 --> 00:12:03.200
for which I'd have to leave my
trillion dollars of options.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.033
And I had to--

00:12:04.033 --> 00:12:04.990
- None of it was vested?

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.149
- Well no, none if it was vested, right?

00:12:07.149 --> 00:12:08.470
It was sort of locked up.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.360
But I had to make, okay,
here's this person who's got

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.120
this huge job making this
decision to join the company

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:15.250
at the exact moment we're
only weeks away from total

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:18.985
imminent decline, and yet I
have to decide to walk away.

00:12:18.985 --> 00:12:21.080
And I made the right choice.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.703
I made more money selling
the gear on eBay, you know,

00:12:22.703 --> 00:12:25.000
that I did at the
company with the options.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.440
But the point of that story
is trust your instincts.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.690
Like, don't outsource
your judgment to somebody

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:30.795
who has less facts.

00:12:30.795 --> 00:12:33.092
- What was the max value of
all those stocks on paper?

00:12:33.092 --> 00:12:35.140
- I'm in a few million
dollars at this point.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.240
But I never tried to get too
close to it because it never

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.390
happened--
- But that was like, you know.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:39.890
- No, I won!

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:40.723
I was rich!

00:12:40.723 --> 00:12:41.556
- And then went to zero.

00:12:41.556 --> 00:12:42.389
- [Matt] Yeah, went to zero.

00:12:42.389 --> 00:12:43.840
- It's gonna happen to
so many people soon.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:44.920
Oh, it's gonna be interesting.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:48.100
What's interesting about
the collapse of 01 and 07

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:51.033
was there wasn't a
majority of social media.

00:12:51.033 --> 00:12:54.330
What I'm unbelievably worried
about, and why I keep talking

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.850
about it and why I will
continue talking about it

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.680
basically everyday, is
we have a bunch of people

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.795
that are posturing of success
on Instagram and Facebook

00:13:01.795 --> 00:13:04.410
and Twitter every single
day, and they're all,

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:07.819
and 99% are gonna lose 'cause
they're over-leveraged,

00:13:07.819 --> 00:13:10.480
which means they're gonna
have to deal with the fact

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.750
of that judgment at scale.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:14.330
Like, you weren't posting.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:18.170
We can't look at your Twitter
from 2002 talking big game

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.530
at being at NoBu, right?

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.427
Because you had two million
in the bank as a 20 something

00:13:23.427 --> 00:13:26.537
in stock, but that's what was happening.

00:13:26.537 --> 00:13:28.374
- But do you think the
cycle's the same now?

00:13:28.374 --> 00:13:29.207
- [Gary] 100%.

00:13:29.207 --> 00:13:30.638
I remember companies were
getting funded Pets.com,

00:13:30.638 --> 00:13:32.268
all this was about Lion Share?

00:13:32.268 --> 00:13:33.101
- Yes, I do.

00:13:33.101 --> 00:13:33.934
- Are people getting funded on My Share?

00:13:33.934 --> 00:13:35.810
- I do believe people who
are claiming on Instagram

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.620
right now that they're
building the next supreme,

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:40.600
or the next Instagram, and
every picture during the summer

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:43.590
is in the Hamptons on a
swan when they go work

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.792
at Chase in two years because
they're out of business,

00:13:46.792 --> 00:13:48.150
that's gonna hurt.

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.090
Yes I do, I think that is a problem.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:52.280
- Okay, what are we gonna
do when that all collapses?

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.140
- Well, we're gonna make a
lot of money because we're

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.740
preparing for that and
are building a machine,

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:00.510
VaynerMedia's built in a
way, like all the margin

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:04.490
I don't deliver is predicated
on capturing the growth

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:07.890
when Poll and Springs goes
from 100 million to 29 million

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.302
dollar budget and they
have to make that work.

00:14:10.302 --> 00:14:14.050
So, you know, but it's
not like I'm like he he he

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:16.440
being secretive, I'm
just, I'm telling people

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:17.400
what I'm doing.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.670
My biggest advice always
to my audience is watch

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:22.740
what I'm doing, not what I'm
saying, because then you'll

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.340
really know what the hell is going on.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:25.173
What's going on?

00:14:25.173 --> 00:14:27.580
I'm eating crow running
this God damn company

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:30.470
because I know how fruitful
it will be when inevitably--

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:31.303
- And that's what I would say.

00:14:31.303 --> 00:14:32.830
I always think the one thing
that you don't give enough

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:35.190
insight into is just how
hard you work as an operator,

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:38.490
how much you spend all your
time, and how right you are

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.880
on trends, business trends.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:42.810
I've got one incredible
insight, not to blow your smoke,

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:43.643
but you are.

00:14:43.643 --> 00:14:45.550
And from that first time
we met in the bagel store,

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:46.926
alright, that's when I said I like Gary.

00:14:46.926 --> 00:14:48.421
- Why don't we tell that story I think?

00:14:48.421 --> 00:14:49.868
So, let's go quickly, 'cause
I wanna get into some--

00:14:49.868 --> 00:14:50.701
- Yeah, I get bored of the bio story.

00:14:50.701 --> 00:14:53.270
- Juliana, you're with him
during 9/11, that's an episode

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:56.130
within itself, I mean, that
you should be putting content

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:59.960
about, you should put
it just for generations

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:00.793
in the future.

00:15:00.793 --> 00:15:01.626
It's just interesting stuff.

00:15:01.626 --> 00:15:05.640
Like, to be at the hip of
Mayor Giuliani during 9/11

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.245
is one of the unique lives, in America.

00:15:08.245 --> 00:15:09.266
I don't know what else to say.

00:15:09.266 --> 00:15:12.487
It's yours, thus it's
hard for you to see it.

00:15:12.487 --> 00:15:15.560
I don't know how big
that team was or wasn't.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:16.393
But you're talking about 40,
20, help me here, jump in,

00:15:16.393 --> 00:15:21.104
19, 27, what was the core
circle of Team Giuliani on 9/11.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.030
- That's probably yeah, 25 people.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:25.863
I was doing--

00:15:25.863 --> 00:15:26.696
- Think about that.

00:15:26.696 --> 00:15:30.010
Out of 300 plus Americans who,
you know, there's some young

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.820
people who didn't, it's crazy
now to think about how many

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:35.423
people didn't, 9/11, what are
you talking, 17 years ago?

00:15:35.423 --> 00:15:39.560
17 years ago, man, it's crazy
that 22 year olds don't know.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:40.393
- Yeah, they don't, I--

00:15:40.393 --> 00:15:41.370
- [Gary] Like, how old are you?

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:42.210
- [Josiah] 24.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.090
- You know, like--

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:44.770
- [Matt] It starts to take on
a Pearl Harbor quality, right?

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:45.603
- You were here in America?

00:15:45.603 --> 00:15:46.550
- No, I was in Houston.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:47.510
How old are you?

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.240
But in Houston, I'm sorry, I wasn't sure.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:50.413
Like, you were in America on 9/11.

00:15:50.413 --> 00:15:51.290
- [Josiah] Yes, yes I was.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:53.719
- What do you think about when I say 9/11?

00:15:53.719 --> 00:15:55.550
Do you remember anything?

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:56.850
- [Josiah] Hardly anything.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:57.683
- How old are you?

00:15:57.683 --> 00:15:58.516
- [Dustin] 34.

00:15:58.516 --> 00:15:59.810
- Man, you look great.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:00.966
Brooke, how old are you?
(people laughing)

00:16:00.966 --> 00:16:01.799
- [Brooke] I'm 22.

00:16:01.799 --> 00:16:03.056
- 22, you don't--

00:16:03.056 --> 00:16:03.889
- [Matt] Yeah, what do you--

00:16:03.889 --> 00:16:04.722
- [Brooke] I remember it.

00:16:04.722 --> 00:16:05.555
- But how?

00:16:05.555 --> 00:16:06.388
- [Brooke] On the TV.

00:16:06.388 --> 00:16:07.221
- But like, how?

00:16:07.221 --> 00:16:08.770
For you, 9/11 is like--

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:09.930
- Is it like Pearl Harbor?

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:11.623
- It's like Vietnam.
- Or is it like Vietnam, or?

00:16:11.623 --> 00:16:12.843
- It means nothing.

00:16:12.843 --> 00:16:14.592
Like it meant something,
but you were five.

00:16:14.592 --> 00:16:17.510
- [Brooke] My grandmother was
in the room, she's crying.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:19.056
- Right, so that's what you remember.

00:16:19.056 --> 00:16:19.889
- [Brooke] Yeah.

00:16:19.889 --> 00:16:20.965
- But like, it's cra,
anyway, none the less,

00:16:20.965 --> 00:16:25.040
for most of, like it's crazy
to think that you were so close

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:28.094
to probably the signature moment
of the majority of people's

00:16:28.094 --> 00:16:31.000
lives in American History.
- Yeah, I was standing,

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:33.950
I was standing outside when
the first building collapsed.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:35.570
Right, we were setting up to do,

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:38.700
one thing about Giuliani, he
handled a crisis by letting

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:41.750
everybody know that, you
know, it was under control

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.450
and that he was there.
- We got this.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:46.660
- So, right before the first
tower collapsed, I was standing

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:49.320
outside setting up a press
conference so that he could do

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:52.123
what he normally would do,
which was say we've got this.

00:16:52.123 --> 00:16:54.610
And then, it just was
so clear that this was,

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:56.030
this was a war zone.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.000
This is unlike, but in the
beginning, you remember?

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.041
It wasn't clear.

00:16:59.041 --> 00:17:00.128
- Yeah it was like a plane hit--

00:17:00.128 --> 00:17:01.005
- The first report.

00:17:01.005 --> 00:17:01.930
Plane hit, was it a Cesena?

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.690
And so for me, that began
a two year chapter, right?

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.270
Because I was there for that
moment, stayed with Giuliani

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.630
as Press Secretary managing
the global response

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.840
to the attack, which became
its own obligation, right?

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:14.340
How do we telegraph what happened here?

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:16.094
How do we enlist the support of the world?

00:17:16.094 --> 00:17:20.511
And then, I became the first
employee of the rebuilding.

00:17:20.511 --> 00:17:23.820
So I arrived with a cell
phone and a massive federal

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:26.139
budget with a group of
people to start rebuilding.

00:17:26.139 --> 00:17:27.800
I spent two years of my life.

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.533
I moved down there, I would
sit on the roof of my building

00:17:30.533 --> 00:17:34.100
on Chambers Street and write
speeches while everything

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:35.370
was still going on.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:38.050
So, it was just basically,
that day really lasted

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:38.883
for two years.

00:17:38.883 --> 00:17:40.380
That's why it's hard for
me to tell the story 'cause

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:41.730
it's still kind of a blur.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:43.080
Like, I don't totally understand it.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.900
I haven't gone back and
looked at all the footage

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:46.650
and understood the impact--

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:47.675
- And then what happened next?

00:17:47.675 --> 00:17:51.400
- So I did two years, I decided
I was gonna see it through

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.282
until we had a design for the memorial

00:17:53.282 --> 00:17:56.439
and the Freedom Tower
and then I transitioned

00:17:56.439 --> 00:17:58.434
to the New York Jets.

00:17:58.434 --> 00:17:59.267
- That's right.

00:17:59.267 --> 00:18:00.119
- That's where I'm at--

00:18:00.119 --> 00:18:00.952
- So how did that happen?

00:18:00.952 --> 00:18:03.890
- The New York Jets were
looking for somebody to help

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:06.193
them build a stadium
because they never had one.

00:18:06.193 --> 00:18:07.759
- That's here--
- Here, in New York.

00:18:07.759 --> 00:18:08.592
- Literally, no, no.

00:18:08.592 --> 00:18:09.425
Here.
- Oh yeah, here!

00:18:09.425 --> 00:18:11.146
- Like literally here.

00:18:11.146 --> 00:18:11.979
- [Matt] That's right, here.

00:18:11.979 --> 00:18:13.270
- This Hudson Yard's project.

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:15.969
- [Matt] So we waged an epic, you know--

00:18:15.969 --> 00:18:16.847
- Come to war.

00:18:16.847 --> 00:18:17.680
- Yes, battle.

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.550
Spent, both sides, probably
100 million dollars

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:24.250
fighting, Cable Vision
went to war to stop it,

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:25.083
thank you.

00:18:25.083 --> 00:18:25.950
- This is Gorgardon.
- Right, exactly.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.260
So that lasted probably two years.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:29.650
And, ultimately--

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:30.556
- How close did it get?

00:18:30.556 --> 00:18:32.090
- It got really close.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.700
We had a majority of the
City Council supporting it.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:36.930
- Uh, for everybody who is
listening, this is obviously

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:38.270
very emotionally for me.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:39.733
The Jets wanted to build--
- Yeah, for you.

00:18:39.733 --> 00:18:41.250
- Way more for me than you.

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:42.758
I don't give a shit how involved you were.

00:18:42.758 --> 00:18:44.896
Emotionally, way more than me.
- Way more, this part

00:18:44.896 --> 00:18:46.970
is emotional for me.

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:50.080
- Fair enough, but we are
literally, literally the office

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:52.350
building we're in right now is in the spot

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:56.570
that was battled over in
the early 2000's about where

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.050
the New York Jets almost
built their stadium,

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:00.400
which would have been in Manhattan.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:03.260
And as somebody who's a Jet's
fan in a town that is very

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:07.070
Giant's out, you can imagine
having the actual stadium

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:08.910
in Manhattan was
obviously very attractive.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:10.708
Now I'm a Jersey boy and I
love that it's out there,

00:19:10.708 --> 00:19:14.630
but it was an attractive
proposition being in Manhattan.

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:16.200
- And it was going to
be home to the Olympics.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:17.700
- That's exactly right.
- Remember, and they killed

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:18.861
the Olympic bit.
- Right, of course.

00:19:18.861 --> 00:19:20.910
- So, it's so hard to explain
the convoluted political

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.777
process but it was a war of television ads

00:19:23.777 --> 00:19:26.347
ads and tens of thousands
of union representatives--

00:19:26.347 --> 00:19:27.210
- Oh it was crazy.
- Fighting, it was crazy.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:28.260
And I ran that fight.

00:19:29.669 --> 00:19:30.780
And then, it died on
the steps of the capital

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:33.000
when Shelly Silver, who
is now going to jail--

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:34.120
- [Gary] Is that true?

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:36.530
- Yeah, yeah, Shelly's been
indicted, convicted, yeah,

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.110
well, see the bitterness lasts.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:38.943
- Yes, yeah of course.
- That's okay,

00:19:38.943 --> 00:19:40.260
I'm gonna hold onto it.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:41.130
And we lost.

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:44.490
Right, and so then, and the
reason why the Jets chose me

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:46.170
is they needed somebody
who had that background

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:47.503
in the city politics--
- I get it.

00:19:47.503 --> 00:19:48.674
- And whatnot.

00:19:48.674 --> 00:19:49.630
- And what did you come in as?

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:52.680
- I come in as like VP of Strategy.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:53.573
- [Gary] And then what?

00:19:53.573 --> 00:19:56.600
- And every other job eventually ending up

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:58.010
as EVP of the business.

00:19:58.010 --> 00:19:59.541
Running the business of the team, right?

00:19:59.541 --> 00:20:01.842
- How long were you with the Jets?

00:20:01.842 --> 00:20:03.260
- Eight years.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:04.503
- Man, that's a real run.

00:20:04.503 --> 00:20:08.090
- I started with the Jets
when I was probably 28?

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.574
- And Hafstra was headquarters,
well, headquarters

00:20:10.574 --> 00:20:11.587
is in Manhattan, but--

00:20:11.587 --> 00:20:14.040
- Well I was in Manhattan,
they were out in Hafstra,

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:15.071
and then--

00:20:15.071 --> 00:20:15.904
- Right, you moved to Florida.

00:20:15.904 --> 00:20:16.737
- Yeah, and then I ran that process.

00:20:16.737 --> 00:20:18.790
Alright, so I ran the
process how to figure out

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:20.796
how to build a practice
facility in New Jersey

00:20:20.796 --> 00:20:24.360
and then relocated in
New Jersey out of anger.

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.040
- And Matt, we met because what?

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.530
- We met because I was
trying to sell you a suite.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.170
You were like, we have
this like pinup at the team

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.680
at the time, who could afford
a suite but isn't buying one?

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:33.513
- Right, Wine Library.

00:20:33.513 --> 00:20:35.930
- Yeah, and you were like,
you know, you were target

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:39.096
number and that bagel store
was a target rich environment,

00:20:39.096 --> 00:20:40.790
right, so I had to come out and see you.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:42.440
- Such an unbelievable
story, really quick.

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:43.770
'Cause I want to get into
other parts of his career

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:45.540
about Matt, I wanna get
into the Shark Tank stuff--

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:46.813
- [Matt] No, I like telling this story.

00:20:46.813 --> 00:20:48.272
- But, it, so go ahead.

00:20:48.272 --> 00:20:49.490
- It's interesting, no because
it's the Genesis story.

00:20:49.490 --> 00:20:52.400
- I'm running wine library,
this is as me and AJ

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:54.350
are planning VaynerMedia but no body knows

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:56.628
so I'm actively just running Wine Library.

00:20:56.628 --> 00:20:59.260
I get an email from the Jets
which always gets responded to

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:01.320
right away, and the
President wants to meet you,

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.676
and I'm like hell yes,
and we went to Bagel Smith

00:21:03.676 --> 00:21:08.330
on Springfield Ave in
Springfield, New Jersey, yes.

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:09.484
- That's right.

00:21:09.484 --> 00:21:10.640
So I'm going to sell Gary a
suite, which is not something

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.677
that you love to do as an executive.

00:21:12.677 --> 00:21:14.560
So, it's a hard thing to sort of sell.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:17.227
I go to meet Gary, Jeff
Fernandez had asked us to do it,

00:21:17.227 --> 00:21:19.727
and I go to meet Gary in a bagel store.

00:21:19.727 --> 00:21:22.350
And in the first ten
minutes I'm listening to you

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:24.180
with your phonetic energy
and your predictions

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:27.483
and I didn't follow you on Wine Library.

00:21:27.483 --> 00:21:29.880
But I always remember, I think
about the second ten minutes

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.840
right, 'cause that's when
all this magic happened.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:35.280
Gary started telling all these
stories about how the world

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:36.350
was gonna unfold.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.650
And to give you an example,
I started making predictions

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:42.910
that Matt, in another couple
of years, everything is gonna

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:45.410
be about social media and digital content,

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:48.470
but more importantly, content
is gonna be democratized.

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:51.287
And everybody is gonna become
their own content publisher.

00:21:51.287 --> 00:21:54.440
And because of that, these
corporations are like big

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:56.440
battle ships, they're not
gonna be able to manage that

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.760
in house, they're never gonna
have enough sophistication

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:01.560
to figure out how to go
ahead and tap into that.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:03.880
And there's gonna be a new
type of agency that'll be born

00:22:03.880 --> 00:22:06.410
that will figure out how to manage that.

00:22:06.410 --> 00:22:07.507
And I'm the guy to do it.

00:22:07.507 --> 00:22:09.280
Interesting, sounds legit.

00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:12.120
And my brother AJ and I,
as soon as he graduates

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:14.460
'cause he's 12, we're gonna
launch this big company

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:16.330
and I'm gonna be a billionaire,
I'm gonna buy the Jets.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:19.230
You fucking tell everybody
I'm gonna buy the jets, right?

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:22.160
So you know, going back
to pattern recognition

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:24.814
smoking the butt in high school, right?

00:22:24.814 --> 00:22:28.470
Over those years I started
trusting myself that when I see

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.760
a pattern playing out, and
I see somebody speaking

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:32.990
the truth that's got the
courage to get it right,

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:35.190
and it resonates with me, I
don't care if anybody else

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:37.330
in the world knows if
they're right or not.

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:40.780
So, within those second
ten minutes, I was like oh,

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:43.360
Gary is sitting in the stream
of information and data,

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:45.730
he's opening up himself to
the world and is starting

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:47.630
to see the patterns, and he's right.

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:49.269
And I don't know if you remember
too, you started predicting

00:22:49.269 --> 00:22:50.890
I don't know, I think
Facebook is gonna be,

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:52.074
all the things that you--
- I wish I was recording

00:22:52.074 --> 00:22:53.490
that meeting.

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:54.615
- [Matt] Yeah, no--

00:22:54.615 --> 00:22:55.810
- It would look really
good on me right now.

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:56.643
- It really would.

00:22:56.643 --> 00:22:57.830
So with that, and that's
why I tell everybody,

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.230
you know it's cliche, but you
know where the puck is going.

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:02.700
So at that meeting, we did a deal, right?

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:03.533
- [Gary] Yup.

00:23:03.533 --> 00:23:04.366
- A couple things.

00:23:04.366 --> 00:23:06.496
Take a Jet's player who
doesn't deserve to be famous,

00:23:06.496 --> 00:23:08.060
and make him Twitter famous.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.076
- [Gary] That's right.

00:23:09.076 --> 00:23:09.909
- That was mission number one.

00:23:09.909 --> 00:23:12.830
If you succeed, we'll give
you four Jet's tickets

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:14.840
to become the first client,
I know others claim this,

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.690
but I'm gonna claim I
am the first client--

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:17.523
- [Gary] Yes, yup.

00:23:17.523 --> 00:23:19.500
- And two, make me the
guy in sports two gets it.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.578
So, I can get a huge job, right?

00:23:21.578 --> 00:23:22.620
(men laughing)

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:23.530
Total self dealage.

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:25.040
Check, check--
- And check.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:26.080
- [Matt] And none of those early articles

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:26.950
do I credit Gary.
- No, should have.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:28.280
- Which I think you
even framed one of them

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:29.825
so you give me shit about it, right?

00:23:29.825 --> 00:23:30.717
- [Gary] Yes, I did.

00:23:30.717 --> 00:23:31.550
- It was the journal article.

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:32.383
- Yes.

00:23:32.383 --> 00:23:33.510
Matt Higgins master my social media rise

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:34.572
of the New York Jets.

00:23:34.572 --> 00:23:36.495
And I'm reading it, it's
somewhere else else in here

00:23:36.495 --> 00:23:37.580
nope, not in here.

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:39.400
- By the way, even in Fast
Company, I pulled up an article

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:40.912
today--
- I love it.

00:23:40.912 --> 00:23:42.640
- But no, remember we had dinner in Chatum

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:44.350
with Kerry Rhodes,
- With Kerry Rhodes.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:46.050
- Kerry Rhodes, Mr. Hollywood.

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:47.430
- One of my favorite players of all time.

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:48.750
- And you made him--

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:49.583
- Massive!

00:23:50.441 --> 00:23:52.410
Kerry Rhodes, at one point,
was literally the second

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:55.110
or first most followed
football player as a safety

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:56.940
for the New York Jets on Twitter.

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.210
The Jets were the number
one team followed on Twitter

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:01.600
in 2009 and 10, period.

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:03.590
And so yeah, we did a lot of great work.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:04.423
It was a lot of fun.

00:24:04.423 --> 00:24:07.160
I remember my first meeting,
we had just drafted Sanchez,

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:09.700
and I was like I don't
need, like, sports marketing

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:11.330
is like market around the stars.

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:14.100
And I was like, I wanna
make the punter famous.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.300
I wanna make the people
that cover coverage

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:19.030
on special teams, and I still
believe that to this day.

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:21.850
I still believe to this second
the biggest vulnerability

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:24.310
in sports marketing is
they give too much power

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.580
to the individual athlete and
the athlete should be doing

00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:29.520
that, everybody should be doing
what they should be doing,

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:30.650
but anyway, it was a great run.

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:33.070
- But remember how hard it
was in those early days.

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:35.060
When you would get a cease and
decease letter from marketing

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:36.000
on Myspace--
- Of course.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.700
- I think in 2009 I got a
cease and decease letter

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:41.350
from the NFL, like you're not
allowed to market on Myspace,

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.398
right, so it wasn't easy to go ahead.

00:24:43.398 --> 00:24:44.231
- [Gary] No.

00:24:44.231 --> 00:24:45.640
- And players were still
scrppling with am I allowed

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:46.473
to use Twitter?

00:24:46.473 --> 00:24:47.654
- [Gary] 100%.

00:24:47.654 --> 00:24:48.750
- I mean, actually, not even
grappling was even allowed,

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:51.590
right, so we were breaking
down walls back in the day.

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:52.423
- [Gary] We were, we were.

00:24:52.423 --> 00:24:53.615
- To give ourselves credit.

00:24:53.615 --> 00:24:55.370
- And then your career transitioned again.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:56.233
And by the way, I don't
think I've ever told you

00:24:56.233 --> 00:24:57.066
this story.

00:24:57.066 --> 00:25:00.534
I came to a Jets practice
during pre-season

00:25:00.534 --> 00:25:04.000
and you said hey, there's
something brewing in my career,

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:05.548
and I remember thinking--
- I remember thinking,

00:25:05.548 --> 00:25:06.676
do you remember that?
- I remember it very well.

00:25:06.676 --> 00:25:07.845
- [Matt] And we were
standing in the sun light.

00:25:07.845 --> 00:25:08.950
- Uh huh, and I remember
thinking no, no, no, Matt.

00:25:08.950 --> 00:25:10.393
I like you at the Jets.

00:25:10.393 --> 00:25:11.226
- [Matt] Right, right.

00:25:11.226 --> 00:25:12.059
- I like the Jets.

00:25:12.059 --> 00:25:13.258
Yeah, don't leave.

00:25:13.258 --> 00:25:14.460
But you were right.

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:16.191
- Yeah, no I like you.

00:25:16.191 --> 00:25:18.480
I always had the desire
to be an entrepreneur.

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:20.710
And I had all those ideas that
were percolating in my head

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:22.730
and I think you reach
a tipping point, right?

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:25.700
At 2 o'clock in the morning
you're like oh, I can run this.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:27.630
We were talking about
creating a fund together.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:29.650
Gary's got all this great
insight, if only we could

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:30.800
monetize it.

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:34.120
Jessie Deris, our partner in
my PR firm, I knew that he

00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:36.340
needed to be running his own firm.

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:38.470
Just all these ideas, and it just,

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:39.950
it reached a tipping point.

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:42.130
- How long did Ross, Steve
Ross, who now we're gonna

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.910
transition to over to Dolphins
who brought you on board,

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:47.840
how long was he courting
you as an executive?

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:48.850
Was that a quick courtship?

00:25:48.850 --> 00:25:49.683
I don't know this story.

00:25:49.683 --> 00:25:51.870
- Yeah, I think I had
made, I was going through

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:53.067
a lot of personal transitions in my life.

00:25:53.067 --> 00:25:55.160
And I had made the decision like, okay,

00:25:55.160 --> 00:25:57.150
if I don't transition out of this job,

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:57.983
I'm never gonna leave it.

00:25:57.983 --> 00:25:59.410
Like, sometimes it's tough.

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:01.690
Like, when you have a job that
everybody in the world wants,

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:04.000
or a lot of people want,
it's very hard to listen

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:05.414
to your inner voice and say that's great

00:26:05.414 --> 00:26:07.810
that this is the job you
want, but I've run my course--

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:08.960
- Yeah, but you're eight years.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:12.730
- Yeah, eight years and if
the office on the 50 yard line

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:14.350
and the private plane and everything,

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:15.530
